    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the LTI system output by evaluating the continuous-time convolution integral for a given input signal and impulse response.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this lesson, the input signal is written as $x(t)$, the impulse response is written as $h(t)$, and the output signal is written as $y(t)$. The impulse response $h(t)$ describes the LTI system, so once $x(t)$ and $h(t)$ are known, the output is determined by convolution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(x*h)(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This equation says that the output value at time t is the convolution of the input with the impulse response. The variable t is the output time, so the final answer is still a function of t.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To compute that convolution directly, use a dummy integration variable $\\tau$. The input $x(t)$ becomes $x(\\tau)$, and the impulse response $h(t)$ becomes $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The basic workflow is: identify $x(t)$, $h(t)$, and $y(t)$; write the convolution integral; substitute the formulas as $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$; then evaluate the resulting integral for the output. Later sections will practice those moves one at a time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Input, Impulse Response, and Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A causal LTI system is driven by input $x(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$ and has impulse response $h(t)=e$^{-t}$u(t)$. Identify the input signal, impulse response, and output symbol.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by matching each phrase in the statement to its role. The phrase \"driven by input\" names the input signal, so the input is $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phrase \"has impulse response\" names the system's impulse response, so the impulse response is $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The output signal is the unknown response of the LTI system. We name it $y(t)$, and it is the convolution of the input with the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(x*h)(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the labels are: input $x(t)=u(t)-u(t-2)$, impulse response $h(t)=e$^{-t}$u(t)$, and output $y(t)$. This step only identifies the roles; the convolution integral is set up later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing the Output as a Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has input $x(t)=u(t)-u(t-1)$ and impulse response $h(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$. Write the output $y(t)$ as a convolution integral before substituting the formulas.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the system is LTI and the statement gives both the input $x(t)$ and the impulse response $h(t)$. For an LTI system, the output is the convolution of those two signals.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(x*h)(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To write this as an integral, use $\\tau$ as the integration variable. The input is read at $\\tau$, and the impulse response is read at t-$\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)\\to x(\\tau),\\qquad h(t)\\to h(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now place those two factors inside the convolution integral, integrate with respect to $\\tau$, and use infinite bounds for the general continuous-time convolution form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the requested setup. The given formulas for $x(t)$ and $h(t)$ have not been substituted yet; that is the next move after the convolution template is written.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Substituting $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t - \\tau)$ into the Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=u(t)-u(t-1)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$, substitute $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ into the convolution integral for $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the convolution integral formed in the previous step. The new move is to replace the symbolic factors $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ with the matching formulas from the problem.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the input, replace every t in $x(t)=u(t)-u(t-1)$ with $\\tau$. This gives the factor $x(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=u(\\tau)-u(\\tau-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the impulse response, replace every t in $h(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ with t-$\\tau$. This gives the shifted factor $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t-\\tau)=e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now multiply those two substituted factors inside the same integral. Keep the infinite bounds and do not evaluate or simplify the overlap regions yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}[u(\\tau)-u(\\tau-1)]e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the substituted convolution integral. The setup is complete for this section; evaluating the integral is a separate step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "System Output from Input and Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Integral for the Output Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an LTI system with input $x(t)=e$^{-t}$u(t)$ and impulse response $h(t)=u(t)$, the convolution setup on $0<t<1$ is $y(t)$=$\\int$_0^t $e^{-\\tau}$\\,d$\\tau$. Evaluate $y(t)$ on this interval.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The convolution setup and the time region are already given. The task is only to evaluate the definite integral for $0<t<1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-\\tau}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate with respect to $\\tau$. An antiderivative of $e^{-\\tau}$ is -$e^{-\\tau}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int e^{-\\tau}\\,d\\tau=-e^{-\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the upper bound $\\tau$=t and the lower bound $\\tau$=0, then simplify.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left[-e^{-\\tau}\\right]_0^t=-e^{-t}-(-e^0)=1-e^{-t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Attach the stated interval to the result: on $0<t<1$, the output is $y(t)=1-e$^{-t}.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
